1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,962
Four questions, and your clone can
answer about half of one of them.

2
00:00:08,302 --> 00:00:13,353
Your clone is your own copy of the repository,
and a commit is one saved snapshot in

3
00:00:13,393 --> 00:00:18,323
it. Your clone knows two commit IDs, if it
happened to fetch, that is, to download

4
00:00:18,363 --> 00:00:22,263
the server's state, before and
after. It doesn't know who pushed.

5
00:00:22,303 --> 00:00:26,323
The author field of a commit doesn't
say either: anyone can set it.

6
00:00:26,663 --> 00:00:31,522
And there's a second problem. The person
who noticed on Friday evening tried three

7
00:00:31,562 --> 00:00:36,541
repairs before calling you. Each repair
moved refs, the names that point at commits,

8
00:00:36,581 --> 00:00:41,500
and pushed lines down in the reflog, Git's
local record of where each ref has been.

9
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:46,399
One of the repairs ran a cleanup. The
evidence you need is partly gone, and it was

10
00:00:46,439 --> 00:00:50,342
destroyed by the investigation. So
where do the four answers live?

11
00:00:50,382 --> 00:00:52,792
By the end you'll have a source for each.

12
00:00:54,632 --> 00:00:58,177
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:58,217 --> 00:01:03,115
This is the second phase of the method:
preserve. In video 180 it was one command, a

14
00:01:03,155 --> 00:01:06,994
rescue branch, which is a new name
placed on an endangered commit.

15
00:01:07,034 --> 00:01:11,579
Today you learn the four layers of
preservation, what each holds and what each

16
00:01:11,619 --> 00:01:16,458
misses, and you watch a repository being
destroyed on purpose and brought back from

17
00:01:16,498 --> 00:01:20,867
each layer. That demonstration is the
argument: a backup made first turns a

18
00:01:20,907 --> 00:01:23,394
destructive mistake
into a recoverable one.

19
00:01:23,734 --> 00:01:27,182
The second half is about evidence
that isn't in any clone.

20
00:01:27,222 --> 00:01:32,114
GitHub records what reached the server,
who was authenticated when it arrived, and

21
00:01:32,154 --> 00:01:37,045
which rules were evaluated. Nothing in that
half is part of Git, and nothing in it

22
00:01:37,085 --> 00:01:42,037
was run for this course. It's described
from GitHub's documentation as the textbook

23
00:01:42,077 --> 00:01:44,262
read it on the first of October 2026.

24
00:01:44,602 --> 00:01:48,279
From video 79 you know backup
refs and bundles as prevention.

25
00:01:48,319 --> 00:01:52,605
A bundle is one file that holds commits
and the refs that go with them.

26
00:01:52,645 --> 00:01:55,591
Here they're steps of a
procedure, with an order.

27
00:01:57,431 --> 00:01:59,009
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:01:59,349 --> 00:02:00,762
After this video you can:

29
00:02:01,102 --> 00:02:05,836
Preserve a repository's state with a backup
ref, a copy and a bundle, and say what

30
00:02:05,876 --> 00:02:07,117
each keeps and misses.

31
00:02:07,458 --> 00:02:11,889
Show that a backup made first turns a
destructive mistake into a recoverable one.

32
00:02:12,229 --> 00:02:15,660
Name the diagnostic commands
that are not strictly read-only.

33
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,333
List the GitHub-side sources of evidence
the section names and what each records.

34
00:02:20,673 --> 00:02:23,746
Say where the timeline of a
server-side branch comes from.

35
00:02:25,586 --> 00:02:30,319
In one sentence. Before the first
state-changing command, put what you might need

36
00:02:30,359 --> 00:02:32,735
later somewhere that the fix can't reach.

37
00:02:33,075 --> 00:02:37,882
Precisely: four layers. Each holds more
than the previous one and costs more.

38
00:02:38,222 --> 00:02:41,521
A ref under refs/backup/
isn't shown by git branch.

39
00:02:41,561 --> 00:02:46,692
A branch under rescue/ is, which is better
when a colleague continues the work.

40
00:02:46,732 --> 00:02:48,460
Both keep the commit alive.

41
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:53,742
None of the four layers changes the working
tree, the index, HEAD or any existing

42
00:02:53,782 --> 00:02:58,848
ref. The working tree is the files you edit,
the index is the proposed next commit,

43
00:02:58,888 --> 00:03:02,969
and HEAD is Git's note of where you
are. A backup ref adds one ref.

44
00:03:03,009 --> 00:03:06,599
The copy and the bundle are
written outside the repository.

45
00:03:06,939 --> 00:03:10,593
Which layer when. A ref is about
to move: a backup ref, always.

46
00:03:10,633 --> 00:03:15,108
Uncommitted work or an operation in
progress is involved: a copy of the whole

47
00:03:15,148 --> 00:03:19,800
directory. The disk is suspect or the
evidence must leave the machine: a bundle,

48
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:24,198
stored elsewhere. Someone else will ask
what happened: the recorded output.

49
00:03:24,538 --> 00:03:29,201
One warning about the copy. A copy of
a repository contains everything the

50
00:03:29,241 --> 00:03:34,286
repository contains, including credentials
in .git/config URLs and any secret in

51
00:03:34,326 --> 00:03:39,370
history. Treat it with the same care, and
delete it when the incident is closed.

52
00:03:39,710 --> 00:03:43,677
Diagnostic commands that
are not strictly read-only.

53
00:03:43,717 --> 00:03:45,680
Section 29.13 names three.

54
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:52,095
git fsck --lost-found writes
files under .git/lost-found/.

55
00:03:52,435 --> 00:03:57,977
git fetch moves remote-tracking refs, your
clone's records of the server's branches.

56
00:03:58,017 --> 00:04:03,160
With fetch.prune=true or --prune it
deletes those whose server branch is gone,

57
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:07,878
together with their reflogs: the last
local record of a deleted branch.

58
00:04:07,918 --> 00:04:11,333
Use git ls-remote when you
must not change anything.

59
00:04:11,673 --> 00:04:15,623
git status may rewrite the index
to refresh cached stat data.

60
00:04:15,663 --> 00:04:21,116
In a repository you must not touch at all,
use git --no-optional-locks status, which

61
00:04:21,156 --> 00:04:24,125
the Git manual documents
for background tools.

62
00:04:24,465 --> 00:04:29,046
GitHub-side evidence, in one sentence. Your
clone records what you did, and GitHub

63
00:04:29,086 --> 00:04:33,610
records what reached the server, who was
authenticated when it arrived, and which

64
00:04:33,650 --> 00:04:34,793
rules were evaluated.

65
00:04:35,133 --> 00:04:38,574
GitHub, not Git. Nothing in
this part is part of Git.

66
00:04:38,614 --> 00:04:43,631
Git's reflogs are local and aren't shared
with remotes, and GitHub exposes no

67
00:04:43,671 --> 00:04:44,879
server-side reflog.

68
00:04:48,219 --> 00:04:52,563
So the timeline of a server-side
branch doesn't come from Git at all.

69
00:04:52,603 --> 00:04:57,455
It comes from these five sources. None
of the commands was executed here, and

70
00:04:57,495 --> 00:04:59,298
user-interface labels change.

71
00:04:59,638 --> 00:05:05,360
The activity endpoint is documented with
the activity types push, force_push,

72
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:11,347
branch_creation, branch_deletion, pr_merge
and merge_queue_merge, and items that

73
00:05:11,387 --> 00:05:14,789
carry before, after, ref,
timestamp and actor.

74
00:05:15,129 --> 00:05:19,880
How the two sides combine. Git evidence
answers "what is the state and which local

75
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:24,788
command produced it". GitHub evidence answers
"which authenticated account moved the

76
00:05:24,828 --> 00:05:29,054
server's ref, when, from which commit
to which, and did a rule allow it".

77
00:05:29,394 --> 00:05:33,759
A "restore" action inside the Activity
view: only filtering and "Compare changes"

78
00:05:33,799 --> 00:05:37,892
are documented there. The access
requirement and the retention period of the

79
00:05:37,932 --> 00:05:41,319
Activity view aren't stated in
the documentation that was read.

80
00:05:41,659 --> 00:05:46,895
How long GitHub keeps commits that no ref
reaches: no retention period is published.

81
00:05:46,935 --> 00:05:52,172
The recovery recipe, find the old ID, then
create a ref at it through the references

82
00:05:52,212 --> 00:05:57,134
API, is an inference from documented
endpoints, not a documented procedure, and

83
00:05:57,174 --> 00:05:59,395
nothing guarantees that it succeeds.

84
00:05:59,735 --> 00:06:04,782
And one consequence: GitHub-side evidence
expires. Thirty days for events, seven

85
00:06:04,822 --> 00:06:08,533
days for enterprise Git events.
Export it on the first day.

86
00:06:10,373 --> 00:06:15,811
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
a database administrator takes a snapshot

87
00:06:15,851 --> 00:06:21,357
before a migration. The analogy breaks only
in cost: a backup ref takes one second,

88
00:06:21,397 --> 00:06:23,762
so time never justifies skipping it.

89
00:06:24,102 --> 00:06:29,248
For the four layers, think of what each
would let you rebuild after the worst

90
00:06:29,288 --> 00:06:33,626
afternoon. Try it now, on paper,
before I say it. Thirty seconds.

91
00:06:33,666 --> 00:06:39,082
Write four words: record, ref, bundle,
copy. Beside each, write what it gives you

92
00:06:39,122 --> 00:06:39,418
back.

93
00:06:41,758 --> 00:06:46,502
The recorded output lets you tell the story.
The backup ref lets you get the commits

94
00:06:46,542 --> 00:06:50,261
back. The bundle lets you get the
commits back on another machine.

95
00:06:50,301 --> 00:06:54,818
The copy lets you get the afternoon back:
the uncommitted edits, the reflog, the

96
00:06:54,858 --> 00:06:56,071
operation in progress.

97
00:06:56,411 --> 00:06:59,301
For the two kinds of evidence,
think of a building.

98
00:06:59,341 --> 00:07:02,922
Your clone is your own notebook
of what you carried in and out.

99
00:07:02,962 --> 00:07:07,577
GitHub is the log at the front desk: who
badged in, at what time, which door, and

100
00:07:07,617 --> 00:07:11,197
whether a guard waved them through.
Neither replaces the other.

101
00:07:11,237 --> 00:07:13,956
And the front desk doesn't
keep its log forever.

102
00:07:15,796 --> 00:07:20,344
Draw the order with an arrow under the
boxes: anchor first, bundle second.

103
00:07:20,384 --> 00:07:24,312
A bundle holds what refs and HEAD
reach at the moment it's made.

104
00:07:24,352 --> 00:07:27,164
A commit that only a
reflog knows isn't in it.

105
00:07:29,004 --> 00:07:33,722
Step 1: record. 🟢 SAFE: writes
files outside the repository.

106
00:07:34,062 --> 00:07:39,654
Two edits: README.md unstaged,
config/service.yaml staged, meaning added to the

107
00:07:39,694 --> 00:07:45,571
index. The output of the opening commands
is in a file, and each edit is saved as a

108
00:07:45,611 --> 00:07:45,999
patch.

109
00:07:46,339 --> 00:07:51,145
Step 2: backup refs. 🟢 SAFE:
each command adds one ref.

110
00:07:51,485 --> 00:07:57,350
One anchor on the detached HEAD, 075407e,
and one on the published branch tip,

111
00:07:57,390 --> 00:07:57,956
4a03014.

112
00:07:58,296 --> 00:08:03,938
Step 3: a copy. 🟢 SAFE. Predict: will the
copy know that a rebase is in progress?

113
00:08:03,978 --> 00:08:08,919
A rebase copies commits onto a new
base, and this one stopped half-way.

114
00:08:08,959 --> 00:08:10,042
Say it out loud.

115
00:08:14,882 --> 00:08:20,228
It will. The copy is a complete repository:
the same rebase in progress, the same

116
00:08:20,268 --> 00:08:25,681
staged and unstaged files, the same reflog.
The -p keeps modification times, which

117
00:08:25,721 --> 00:08:28,009
spares Git a re-read of every file.

118
00:08:28,349 --> 00:08:30,531
Step 4: a bundle. 🟢 SAFE.

119
00:08:30,871 --> 00:08:35,889
Seven refs, among them the backup refs
created a moment ago and the detached HEAD.

120
00:08:35,929 --> 00:08:37,925
That's why the anchor came first.

121
00:08:38,265 --> 00:08:43,238
Step 5: the worst afternoon. The script now
does on purpose what a panicked sequence

122
00:08:43,278 --> 00:08:47,356
of "fixes" does by accident. Don't
run this on a repository you need.

123
00:08:47,696 --> 00:08:54,432
🔴 DANGEROUS: git reflog expire --expire=now
--all followed by git gc --prune=now.

124
00:08:54,472 --> 00:09:01,041
The five answers. What it changes: it
deletes the reflogs and every unreachable

125
00:09:01,081 --> 00:09:05,308
object, meaning every object
that no name leads to.

126
00:09:05,348 --> 00:09:12,084
What it can destroy: the safety net itself.
Preview: git fsck --unreachable lists

127
00:09:12,124 --> 00:09:13,255
what would go.

128
00:09:13,595 --> 00:09:19,449
Recovery: none. When appropriate: only for
purging sensitive data from a clone after

129
00:09:19,489 --> 00:09:25,273
the secret has been rotated. The other
commands of the sequence: git rebase --abort

130
00:09:25,313 --> 00:09:31,167
is 🟡 and resets the index and the working
tree. And git branch -D and git update-ref

131
00:09:31,207 --> 00:09:32,711
-d delete the anchors.

132
00:09:33,051 --> 00:09:36,067
Predict the last two
outputs. Say them out loud.

133
00:09:40,907 --> 00:09:46,016
git rebase --abort printed nothing, and
the last command shows that the staged and

134
00:09:46,056 --> 00:09:48,215
the unstaged edit are gone with it.

135
00:09:48,555 --> 00:09:54,064
With the anchors deleted, the reflogs emptied
and the objects pruned, 075407e is no

136
00:09:54,104 --> 00:09:59,547
longer an object in this repository. This
is the point of no return of Chapter 13,

137
00:09:59,587 --> 00:10:01,218
reached in five commands.

138
00:10:01,558 --> 00:10:03,688
Step 6: back from the bundle.

139
00:10:04,028 --> 00:10:08,735
The bundle returns the three commits. The
new reflog has one line: the history of

140
00:10:08,775 --> 00:10:10,844
how the ref moved isn't in a bundle.

141
00:10:11,184 --> 00:10:16,543
Step 7: back from the copy. Predict what
the copy still has that the bundle didn't.

142
00:10:16,883 --> 00:10:21,433
The copy returns everything, including
the two uncommitted edits and the rebase

143
00:10:21,473 --> 00:10:21,781
state.

144
00:10:22,121 --> 00:10:22,911
The handover.

145
00:10:23,251 --> 00:10:28,261
A note from a colleague: a backport to a
release branch, a conflict resolved by

146
00:10:28,301 --> 00:10:33,567
hand, and "please finish". A backport copies
a fix to a maintenance line, here with

147
00:10:33,607 --> 00:10:38,234
git cherry-pick, which applies one
commit's change to the current branch.

148
00:10:38,274 --> 00:10:43,284
The note contains intent that no Git
command can show: on this release line the

149
00:10:43,324 --> 00:10:46,608
threshold must stay 0.50,
and the limit becomes 128.

150
00:10:46,648 --> 00:10:51,019
git status confirms a cherry-pick
in progress with one unmerged path.

151
00:10:51,359 --> 00:10:52,326
The diagnosis.

152
00:10:52,666 --> 00:10:58,200
CHERRY_PICK_HEAD, a sequencer todo with two
picks, three stages for guard.yaml, and

153
00:10:58,240 --> 00:11:03,842
a combined diff that shows the colleague's
hand-made resolution in the working tree.

154
00:11:03,882 --> 00:11:09,349
That resolution exists in no object. Quick
quiz: which layer of preservation would

155
00:11:09,389 --> 00:11:13,043
keep it? A, the backup ref.
B, the bundle. C, the copy.

156
00:11:13,083 --> 00:11:13,849
Your answer?

157
00:11:21,689 --> 00:11:27,032
C, the copy. A ref and a bundle keep commits,
and this resolution is in no commit.

158
00:11:27,072 --> 00:11:29,067
The copy keeps the file itself.

159
00:11:29,407 --> 00:11:30,235
The preservation.

160
00:11:30,575 --> 00:11:34,687
All four layers, in order: the record,
the anchor, the copy, the bundle.

161
00:11:34,727 --> 00:11:37,801
And one more copy with a
different purpose: rehearsal.

162
00:11:37,841 --> 00:11:42,414
That's the accident investigator's advantage
from video 180: a repository can be

163
00:11:42,454 --> 00:11:45,989
copied in seconds, so you can
rehearse the repair on the copy.

164
00:11:46,329 --> 00:11:50,222
Open your own practice repository
in a browser, in the normal way.

165
00:11:50,262 --> 00:11:54,631
The interface changes, the lab's text
and the linked documentation are the

166
00:11:54,671 --> 00:11:57,670
reference, and nothing was
captured by the authors.

167
00:11:57,710 --> 00:12:02,556
This is only to show where one instrument
is. Find the repository's Activity view,

168
00:12:02,596 --> 00:12:04,999
wherever the current interface places it.

169
00:12:05,339 --> 00:12:09,863
In it, find by function: the filter for
the branch, the filter for the activity

170
00:12:09,903 --> 00:12:14,485
type, which the documentation lists as
direct pushes, pull request merges, force

171
00:12:14,525 --> 00:12:19,107
pushes, branch creations and branch
deletions, and on one entry the control that

172
00:12:19,147 --> 00:12:23,961
compares the before and after commit. That's
all. The documentation doesn't describe

173
00:12:24,001 --> 00:12:27,312
a restore action there, and
this course doesn't claim one.

174
00:12:29,152 --> 00:12:30,800
Five mistakes to watch for.

175
00:12:31,140 --> 00:12:36,201
Skipping preservation because the fix is
"quick". Root cause: the cost of a backup

176
00:12:36,241 --> 00:12:41,054
ref is one second, and the cost of a
wrong fix without one is work that exists

177
00:12:41,094 --> 00:12:41,863
nowhere else.

178
00:12:42,203 --> 00:12:47,134
Making a bundle before anchoring. Root
cause: a bundle holds what refs and HEAD

179
00:12:47,174 --> 00:12:50,407
reach; a commit known only
to a reflog is not in it.

180
00:12:50,747 --> 00:12:54,147
Relying on a backup ref when
uncommitted work is involved.

181
00:12:54,187 --> 00:12:59,130
Root cause: a ref preserves commits; the
index, the working tree and operation state

182
00:12:59,170 --> 00:13:00,850
are preserved only by a copy.

183
00:13:01,190 --> 00:13:04,588
Running git gc or tidying
branches during an incident.

184
00:13:04,628 --> 00:13:09,746
Root cause: reflogs and unreachable objects
are the evidence, and cleanup removes

185
00:13:09,786 --> 00:13:10,637
exactly those.

186
00:13:10,977 --> 00:13:15,305
Looking for "who pushed" in the commit.
Root cause: author and committer are text

187
00:13:15,345 --> 00:13:19,510
set by the client; the authenticated
account is recorded only on the platform.

188
00:13:21,350 --> 00:13:26,080
Now, out of the lab. main of a model-serving
repository moved backwards on Friday

189
00:13:26,120 --> 00:13:30,260
evening. The on-call engineer doesn't
start by moving it forward again.

190
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:35,244
In her own clone, which fetched in the
morning and again at night, git reflog show

191
00:13:35,284 --> 00:13:39,928
origin/main gives the two IDs: where the
remote-tracking branch was, and where the

192
00:13:39,968 --> 00:13:44,670
forced update put it. She anchors the old
ID with a backup ref before anything else

193
00:13:44,710 --> 00:13:49,011
and records the output. That's the Git
half: the state, and the two commits.

194
00:13:49,351 --> 00:13:54,230
The GitHub half answers the rest. The
Activity view filtered by force pushes names

195
00:13:54,270 --> 00:13:58,848
the authenticated account and the time.
Rule Insights says whether a ruleset,

196
00:13:58,888 --> 00:14:03,887
GitHub's named list of rules for certain
refs, evaluated that update and whether the

197
00:14:03,927 --> 00:14:08,686
actor bypassed it. The audit log shows
whether a rule was edited shortly before.

198
00:14:08,726 --> 00:14:13,424
She exports what she finds the same
evening, because the sources have different

199
00:14:13,464 --> 00:14:15,164
lifetimes and some are short.

200
00:14:15,504 --> 00:14:19,967
On Monday she can answer all four questions
of the hook, each with its source.

201
00:14:20,007 --> 00:14:24,817
There are your four answers. And the repair
itself, restoring the ref, was done from

202
00:14:24,857 --> 00:14:29,320
the anchored ID with a lease, a forced
push that succeeds only if the server's

203
00:14:29,360 --> 00:14:31,456
branch is still where she expects it.

204
00:14:33,296 --> 00:14:37,661
Your turn. Do Lab 35.3, "Preserve
evidence, then fix", in

205
00:14:37,701 --> 00:14:43,998
lab-manual/m35-diagnosis-method.md. In this
sitting, do it up to and including the

206
00:14:44,038 --> 00:14:45,002
preservation.

207
00:14:45,342 --> 00:14:49,891
Before you preserve, write down for this
repository what each of the four layers

208
00:14:49,931 --> 00:14:54,710
would hold and what it would miss, and in
particular where the colleague's hand-made

209
00:14:54,750 --> 00:14:59,528
resolution would survive. Then preserve,
and check your prediction by looking inside

210
00:14:59,568 --> 00:15:04,118
the copy and at the list of refs in the
bundle. Leave the fix for after the next

211
00:15:04,158 --> 00:15:06,584
video, or attempt it
now and compare later.

212
00:15:06,924 --> 00:15:13,378
The challenge is Exercise 12.9, Level
4, "A branch that looks like main", in

213
00:15:13,418 --> 00:15:17,138
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

214
00:15:20,978 --> 00:15:26,144
Answer out loud. A strong answer treats
the three as layers with increasing reach

215
00:15:26,184 --> 00:15:31,028
and cost, and for each gives both lists,
kept and missed, in concrete terms:

216
00:15:31,068 --> 00:15:36,105
commits, reflogs, the index, uncommitted
files, operation state, configuration.

217
00:15:36,145 --> 00:15:41,246
It says in which situation each is the
right choice, and why the order of two of

218
00:15:41,286 --> 00:15:46,516
them matters. It mentions what none of them
holds, which is anything that only the

219
00:15:46,556 --> 00:15:51,336
server has. A complete answer adds the
care a copy needs, because of what a

220
00:15:51,376 --> 00:15:53,650
repository can contain besides code.

221
00:15:55,490 --> 00:15:56,417
Let's land this.

222
00:15:56,757 --> 00:16:01,284
Before the first state-changing command,
preserve: record the output, anchor with a

223
00:16:01,324 --> 00:16:05,575
ref, and for uncommitted work or an open
operation copy the whole directory; a

224
00:16:05,615 --> 00:16:08,216
bundle when the evidence
must leave the machine.

225
00:16:08,556 --> 00:16:13,739
A backup ref keeps commits; a copy keeps
everything local; a bundle keeps what refs

226
00:16:13,779 --> 00:16:15,627
and HEAD reach and no reflogs.

227
00:16:15,967 --> 00:16:21,927
git fsck --lost-found, git fetch, especially
with pruning, and git status are not

228
00:16:21,967 --> 00:16:23,334
strictly read-only.

229
00:16:23,674 --> 00:16:28,008
Git records the state and the local commands;
GitHub records which account moved a

230
00:16:28,048 --> 00:16:31,528
server ref, when, from where to
where, and what the rules decided.

231
00:16:31,868 --> 00:16:36,592
GitHub-side evidence has limits and lifetimes,
some facts about it are unverified,

232
00:16:36,632 --> 00:16:39,090
and it should be exported
on the first day.

233
00:16:40,930 --> 00:16:46,249
Read sections 29.7 and 29.8. Then write,
for your own team's main repository, one

234
00:16:46,289 --> 00:16:51,674
line per GitHub-side source: can you read
it, and how far back does it go for your

235
00:16:51,714 --> 00:16:55,445
plan? Where you don't know,
write how you would find out.

236
00:16:55,485 --> 00:17:00,672
The next video ranks fixes by what they
can destroy and defines what "verified"

237
00:17:00,712 --> 00:17:01,069
means.

238
00:17:01,409 --> 00:17:05,815
You can now put evidence out of a fix's
reach in four layers, and say which

239
00:17:05,855 --> 00:17:10,616
questions only GitHub can answer. Practise
the preservation in the lab before you

240
00:17:10,656 --> 00:17:14,233
move on. Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

241
00:17:14,273 --> 00:17:15,655
See you in the next one.
